### Main goals
The paper develops a general lifting principle for symmetry-invariant, finite-dimensional subspaces appearing in sheaf cohomology. Given a short exact sequence of equivariant sheaves and its associated long exact cohomology sequence, it asks when a finite-dimensional invariant subspace in one cohomology group can be represented—or lifted—by an invariant finite-dimensional subspace in an earlier term. The second goal is to use this abstract result to obtain invariant representatives in spaces of currents.

### Methods
The argument combines:

- the long exact sequence in sheaf cohomology;
- equivariance of the connecting and comparison maps;
- finite-dimensional representation theory and invariant complements or controlled extensions;
- cohomological obstruction analysis, identifying the connecting homomorphism as the obstruction to lifting;
- sheaf resolutions by differential forms, distributions, or currents, which turn an abstract cohomology class into an analytic representative.

After isolating the obstruction and showing that it vanishes—or can be handled on the chosen finite-dimensional invariant sector—the paper constructs a lift compatible with the acting group or operator. It then applies the same mechanism to complexes of currents, producing invariant closed currents representing invariant cohomology classes, sometimes simultaneously for an entire finite-dimensional invariant family.

### Significance
The main contribution is a bridge between **cohomological invariance** and **invariance at the level of representatives**. An invariant cohomology class does not automatically have an invariant form or current representing it, especially when averaging is unavailable, as for noncompact groups or dynamically expanding operators. The lifting theorem supplies a systematic substitute for averaging.

For invariant currents, this is important because currents retain geometric and dynamical information that abstract cohomology classes do not. The result therefore provides a reusable tool in geometry and dynamics: it turns finite-dimensional invariant cohomological data into concrete invariant analytic objects and clarifies that the only essential difficulties are encoded by the relevant connecting maps in the long exact sequence.
